course: dictionary
lesson: 13522
title: Impenetrable
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Impenetrable is a long word, but you can break it into parts and use the clues around it. It means impossible to get through, into or see through. «The forest was impenetrable, with thick trees and tangled bushes everywhere.» Nobody could walk through that forest easily, so the words around it show the meaning.
explain: The same word can describe a text. «The science book was impenetrable, full of hard words no child could follow.» If you cannot understand something, however hard you try, it is impenetrable to you. The clue is the words «hard words», which tell you the book is impossible to understand.
explain: Look for word partners. An impenetrable darkness is so black you cannot see anything, and an impenetrable defence is so strong that nothing gets past it. Always try the swap: put impossible to get through in place of the hard word and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Impenetrable means impossible to get through, into or see through.
fact: Impenetrable can also mean impossible to understand, like a very difficult text.
fact: Phrases you may meet are impenetrable jungle, impenetrable darkness and impenetrable defence.
fact: A material that water or light cannot pass through can be called impenetrable.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The jungle was impenetrable, so the explorers had to cut a new path.» What does impenetrable mean here? | Very small and quiet | Easy to see across | *Impossible to get through | Full of friendly animals
exercise: «The professor's report was impenetrable, full of words nobody knew.» What does impenetrable suggest? | Very funny and full of jokes | Brightly coloured | Short and easy to read | *Impossible to understand
exercise: Which phrase describes a defence so strong that nothing can get past it? | bright sunshine | *impenetrable defence | gentle breeze | sweet music
test: «The darkness in the cave was impenetrable, and Sam could not see his own hand.» What does impenetrable mean? | *Too dark to see through | Full of gold coins | Easy to walk through | Warm and cosy
test: Which thing is most likely to be described as impenetrable? | A bright, sunny beach | *A thick stone wall that nothing can pass through | A friendly puppy | A small paper cup
exam: Mia says, «Her maths book was impenetrable to me.» What does she mean? | She found the book funny | *She could not understand it at all | She read it quickly and enjoyed it | She lost the book in the park
